## 17. FIRESTORE SECURITY RULES

Generate complete Firestore Security Rules as part of the project.

Rules must enforce:
- Public visitors can read only published, non-deleted posts.
- Public visitors can read public website settings and categories as needed.
- Public visitors cannot create, edit, or delete blog posts.
- Only the authorized administrator can create, update, or delete posts.
- Only the authorized administrator can manage categories and website settings.
- Only the authorized administrator can access private dashboard data.
- Public visitors cannot read contact submissions or subscriber records.
- No unauthenticated write access to administrative collections.
- Deleted and draft posts are not exposed through public queries.

Do not assume that hiding dashboard links protects Firestore data.

Use Firebase Authentication's request.auth and trusted authorization checks in the rules. If using custom claims or a dedicated administrator allowlist, document how they are provisioned securely.

Ensure that public reads of individual articles cannot bypass the published-status requirement.

Where a public form needs writes, narrowly scope the permitted operation and validate the data. Never grant unrestricted public writes to a collection.

Include the rules file in the generated project and explain how to publish it in Firebase Console.

